HTTPS for the writing aids

Collabora Office reaches the writing aid services, the remote sentence
checkers and the translation providers, over HTTPS only: a base URL that
starts with http:// is never asked. The services run beside it in
grammalecte/, languagetool/ and libretranslate/ speak plain HTTP, so their
compose.yaml files put an nginx front before each, and this directory holds
what the fronts share:

- make-certs.sh makes a private certificate authority and a certificate it
  signs for the service's host name, in ./certs of the service's directory,
  on the first start. It also writes ca-bundle.crt, the usual certificate
  authorities with the private one added. The files belong to the owner of
  the service's directory, so they can be replaced or deleted without root.

- front.conf.template is the nginx configuration of a front: it serves
  https://<host> with that certificate and passes the requests on to the
  service, on a network that only the front and the service share. It
  passes on only the paths Collabora Office asks for, which the TLS_PATHS
  environment variable of the front lists as a regular expression, as in
  TLS_PATHS=/v2/check|/v2/languages, and answers any other path with 404.

Collabora Office is given ca-bundle.crt as its certificate bundle, twice:
at /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt for the server, and at the same path
under /opt/cool/systemplate for the document processes, which run in jails
made from that template and do the checking and the translating. Without
the second, the server finds the service but the documents cannot reach it.
Collabora Office verifies the certificate, so leave ssl_verification on.

To use certificates of your own, put <host>.crt, <host>.key and the ca.crt
that signed them in ./certs before the first start; delete ./certs to have
new ones made.

Without Docker, a certificate authority of your own goes into the system's
bundle, as the distribution does it (update-ca-certificates,
update-ca-trust), and then into the jails' template, which
coolwsd-systemplate-setup refreshes (the Debian packages run it after every
apt run).
